is exquisite sculpture portrays Lord Shiva seated sincerely with his consort Uma (Parvati) by his side and their young child Karthikeya, also known as Skanda between them. \nThis composition radiates divine harmony and familial unity.  This family depiction of Shiva originated during the 6th-8th centuries CE during the period of the Pallava and it is a cherished theme in South Indian art. \nLord Shiva is seated in sukhasana pose, with his left leg folded. His upper two hands hold an elephant goad in the right and a deer in the left. His lower right hand performs the pataka-hasta mudra and the lower left hand performs the kapittha mudra. \n\n\nXLI-52 Nataraja\nNataraja from the 14th century Vijayanagara period, Karnataka. It is a bit distinct from the familiar image of Shiva performing the Ananda Tandava. This four handed Shiva holds the damaru is in his right hand while in the upper left hand he holds the eternal flame, the lower right hand is held in the Abhaya mudra with a cobra entwining the forearm and the left hand is lowered elegantly in the ‘gaja-hasta’ posture. A begging bowl hangs from his left shoulder. His left leg is sculpted upward to the rhythm of a cosmic dance and his right foot presses down on apasmarapurusha, who symbolizes spiritual ignorance. \nThe prabhavali in this image arises from the mouth of two ‘makaras’ or sea creatures on either side rather than emerging directly from the lotus pedestal. The elaborate floral outcrop on either side of Shiva’s head replaces the usual flowing hair. \nThe Vijayanagara kings of southern India reigned from the 14th to 16th centuries and encouraged the metal workers’ art.  \n\nACQ- 67-17-2 - Standing Parsvanatha \nThe Standing Bronze Figure of Parsvanath, dating back to the 18th century, is an exquisite representation of the 23rd Tirthankara in Jainism. Cast in bronze, the figure depicts Parsvanath in the ‘kayotsarga’ posture, with serene facial features.  A nine-hooded serpent forms a canopy over his head. \nHe is believed to have existed 250 years before Mahavira. Known for his compassion and wisdom, Parsvanath renounced his royal life at the age of 30 to embrace asceticism. According to Jain legend, Dharanendra, the serpent protected Parsvanath from a storm caused by a demon and henceforth is a distinct iconographical element in his representation.  \n\n\n\n\n\nACQ-64-39 - Vishnu (9th century) \n\nEarliest bronze figure exhibited in this gallery is this standing figure of Lord Vishnu from 9th century, Tamil Nadu. The four armed god holds chakra in his back right hand and sankha in his back left hand, the front right hand is in Abhayamudra and the left hand is placed on the waist. His tall headgear also known as kireetamakuta is an iconographical attribute of the Pallava dynasty. The yajnopavita is flat and broad. His face is serene with gentle smile and his eyes are almond shaped. He is adorned with necklace, armlet and anklets. His thin legs are draped in cloth from waist. \n\n\u0010\u0012\u001c\n\u0011XLII-100 – Standing Parvati\n\nA 13th century bronze sculpture of Parvathi, the consort of Shiva. She stands on a pedestal, her weight resting largely on the left leg while her right leg is bent at the knee. The pose gives the figure the gracefulness of a dancer. She is ornamented from crown to toe and her hair is arranged in an elaborate conical shape ending in a top knot. Shiva’s symbol, the crescent moon, is a part of the coiffure. Her lower body is draped in a garment with floral patterns. She wears a necklace and a warrior-like cross belt across her torso. \nPallava metal workers in southern India started casting bronzes in the 8th century AD. The most creative period of icon production in south India was during the Chola period from the 9th to 13th century.\n"},{"ty":"as","st":"document","si":0,"ei":0,"sm":{"ds_pw":595.3,"ds_lhs":1,"ds_rtd":"mso-rtd.0","ds_ulhfl":false,"ds_mh":35.4,"ds_ph":841.9,"ds_mf":35.4}},{"ty":"ae","et":"list","id":"kix.list.1","epm":{"le_nb